1232 0 obj
  << /ActualText (I think the built environment is something that structures all of our lives\056 And\054 yes\054 the architect\031s role in shaping that environment is becoming increasingly marginalised\056\0503\051 We need to begin looking for alternative ways to affect it \055 moving beyond the making and shaping of a building as a merely physical object \055 because for the most part\0504\051  that building is defined by areas outside the remit of the traditional architect\056 It is shaped primarily by the forces that govern the property developer or the real estate agent\056\nThe way we start to affect and shape those forces is by guiding and instigating the cultures that exist around them\056 With fiction we can start to shape those cultures \055 and in doing so we have a much larger reach than the making and shaping of any singular building\056 It is through this paradigm shift in the realignment of urban culture that we\047re going to get substantial change\056 Causing the architect to operate as a cultural agent as opposed to a building agent\054 in my mind\054 is the most effective way we can play a role in shaping our futures\056\nTraditionally it is through fiction that cultures have shared and disseminated ideas\056 It is a shame that architects aren\047t better at engaging people in those mediums and aren\031t better at disseminating the really urgent and critical things that we speak about as practitioners but are unable to communicate as individuals\056 Fiction in this sense is a really powerful medium\056)
     /K [ 39 ]
     /P 1233 0 R
     /Pg 1154 0 R
     /S /Span
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
endobj

